            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest major news is that NASA’s Perseverance rover has been studying very ancient rocks on the rim of Jezero Crater and found evidence that a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of layered bedrock, called the “Broom Point member,” was likely built by repeated asteroid impacts more than 3.9 billion years ago【6:1†source】. NASA says these rocks are among the oldest terrain ever examined by a Mars rover and could preserve a record from one of the solar system’s most violent early periods【6:1†source】.\n\nPerseverance also recently hit a mobility milestone: it has driven the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km — reaching that mark on sol 1,890 of its mission. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter captured the rover and its tracks from orbit around the time of the milestone【6:0†source】.\n\nNASA’s own Mars news feed also lists several recent Perseverance updates from July 2026, including a sweeping view of Broom Point, an orbital map of Perseverance’s trip there, and the impact-record findings【6:2†source】. In short: Perseverance is now working beyond Jezero Crater’s rim, examining some of the oldest rocks it has encountered while continuing to rack up distance across Mars.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-perseverance-rover-reads-record-of-ancient-mars-impacts/",
